{-
   `data qualified` - `doc/spec/declarations.md`'s qualified records.

   The keyword takes a spelling away rather than adding one: `Record.Constructor` works for every
   record, and `qualified` is what stops the bare name working *as well*. It is for types whose
   constructor names are too generic to occupy the module namespace, which is exactly the case
   `lib/Date/Format.yana` is - a `TimeDetail` with `Micros` and `Millis` beside a `TimeUnit` with
   the same two, where only one of them can have the module's `Micros`.

   Both stages, because the point is that they agree. The ownership stage read the flag nowhere and
   registered every constructor in the module namespace, so the two declarations below collided and
   `Date`, `File`, `Process` and `Test` were unreachable behind the report.

   There is **no exception for a constructor spelled like its type**: `Wrapped.Wrapped(7)` below
   reads oddly and is what the rule says, since the word is about the module namespace and not about
   which name a constructor happens to have. A **newtype** is not that case even though it is spelled
   `alias qualified` - there the word selects the form rather than the namespace, and its constructor
   is the type's own name with no other spelling available, so it stays reachable bare.
-}

-- The unqualified declaration, which keeps the bare names.
data Detail = NoTime | Minutes | Seconds | Millis

-- And the qualified one, whose constructors are three of the same words. Nothing collides, because
-- these are reachable only as `Unit.Minutes`.
data qualified Unit
  = @value(0) Nanos
  | @value(1) Millis
  | @value(2) Seconds
  | @value(3) Minutes

-- A single-constructor qualified record, whose constructor carries the type's own name and is still
-- out of the namespace: `qualified` is about where the name lives, not about what it is.
data qualified Wrapped = Wrapped(Int)

fn detailOf(d: Detail) -> Int = match d:
    NoTime: 0
    Minutes: 1
    Seconds: 2
    Millis: 3

fn unitOf(u: Unit) -> Int = match u:
    Unit.Nanos: 10
    Unit.Millis: 20
    Unit.Seconds: 30
    Unit.Minutes: 40

-- The bare name still reaches `Detail`'s, which is the half `qualified` did not touch.
fn bare() -> Int = detailOf(Millis) + detailOf(Seconds)

fn qualifiedName() -> Int = unitOf(Unit.Millis) + unitOf(Unit.Seconds)

fn wrapped() -> Int = match Wrapped.Wrapped(7): Wrapped.Wrapped(v) -> v

-- 3 + 2, then 20 + 30, then 7.
fn main() -> Int = bare() + qualifiedName() + wrapped()
